 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "WEST: Bletchley, Milton Keynes and Oxford. EAST: Bedford, Sandy and Cambridge. This was the Varsity Line. The trains would take you between the two universities, in the end with a change at Bletchley and a long wait."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "A railway that once ran 77 miles from Oxford to Cambridge, by way of Bletchley and Bedford, so that dons, undergraduates and the occasional spy could travel between the two universities. Most of it closed in the late 1960s. Parts survive in use, and the missing parts are being rebuilt as East West Rail, which is why you will find a cycle track and a lot of men in hi-vis.",
   "plaque": "w55e_varsity_line",
   "title": "The Varsity Line"
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "THE GREAT OUSE. The fourth-longest river in the UK. It rises in Northamptonshire, wanders through Bedford and Ely, and reaches the Wash at King's Lynn after about 140 miles of not being in a hurry."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 30,
   "text": "Two enormous airship hangars stand on the Bedfordshire flat. The first was built in 1917 and the second, bigger, in 1927, for the government's R100 and R101 airships. The R101 left Cardington for India in October 1930 and crashed in France on the way. Forty-eight of the fifty-four people aboard died. Britain gave up airships, and the sheds have since been used for storage, research and film sets.",
   "plaque": "w55e_cardington",
   "title": "The Cardington Sheds"
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "Bunyan, a tinker from Elstow, was jailed in Bedford for preaching without a licence in 1660 and spent most of the next twelve years in the county gaol. His great book, The Pilgrim's Progress, grew out of those years and came out in 1678. It has never been out of print. The Bunyan Meeting in Bedford stands where his congregation met.",
   "plaque": "w55e_bunyan",
   "title": "John Bunyan"
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "EAST: Sandy, the Lodge, and Cambridge in the distance. The RSPB's headquarters is at The Lodge, Sandy. Expect a lot of binoculars and a very good flapjack."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "CONCRETE COWS. Milton Keynes, to the west, has had its famous concrete cows since 1978. They have been moved about the town and painted more than once. They have never produced any milk."
  }
 ],
